        <notes>
            <note>I doubt that this book was ever sold separately. The only copy outside of </note>
            <note rend="italic">The Polite Gamester </note>
            <note>it at the British Library. See the discussion in </note>
            <note url="whist.d.5.xml">Whist.D.5</note>
            <note>. </note>
            <note rend="paragraph"/>
            <note>It is a nearly line-for-line resetting of </note>
            <note url="quadrille.d.1.xml">Quadrille.D.1</note>
            <note> and it seems likely that the advertisement on A1v, including stale prices, is
                thoughtlessly copied from that work. By 1752, the Ewings had lowered the price of </note>
            <note rend="italic">Whist </note>
            <note>from 6½ d. to 4d. (and included </note>
            <note url="memory.d.2.xml">Memory.D.2*</note>
            <note> as well. They also lowered the price of </note>
            <note rend="italic">The Polite Gamester </note>
            <note> from a British half crown to a British shilling. Compare the advertisement on A1v
                of </note>
            <note url="quadrille.d.1.xml">Quadrille.D.1</note>
            <note> with the contemporary newspaper advertisement quoted in </note>
            <note url="whist.d.4.xml">Whist.D.4</note>
            <note>.</note>
        </notes>
